  "output": "I now have a complete picture of the codebase. Here's my honest assessment.\n\n## Context first\n\nThis is a very early-stage Next.js app (~35 source files, no `runs`/`conflicts`/`rules`/`audit` pages built yet — just Sources, landing, and get-started). It's built almost entirely of **Server Components** with zero client-side data fetching so far; a code comment in `lib/server/sources.ts:9` even states TanStack Query is *reserved* for future run-status polling and isn't wired up yet. That means most of the categories you asked about (N+1 queries across many endpoints, React re-render storms, cache layers, memory leaks in long-lived client state) genuinely don't exist yet — there's nothing to point at without inventing problems. I'd rather tell you that than pad the report.\n\nThat said, I found real, actionable items:\n\n### 1. Redundant computation in `countSourcesBySide` (`lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`)\nIt fetches all rows, Zod-parses every one, then does **three separate full-array `.filter()` passes** just to produce three counts.\n\n```ts\n// current — 1 query + full zod parse + 3 O(n) passes over JS objects\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nFine at current scale, but it's the kind of thing that should be a single pass (or a SQL aggregate) rather than re-scanning:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return all.reduce(\n    (acc, s) => {\n      if (s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\") acc.crm++;\n      else if (s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\") acc.warehouse++;\n      else acc.synthetic++;\n      return acc;\n    },\n    { crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 },\n  );\n}\n```\nOr better, push the aggregation into Postgres with `count()` + `groupBy(sources.kind)` once this table isn't tiny — avoids pulling and parsing every row just to count them.\n\n### 2. No request-level dedup on `listSources()` — worth adding preemptively\n`listSources()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:13`) isn't wrapped in React's `cache()`. Right now it's only called once per render tree, so there's no duplicate query today — but as more pages/layouts need the source list (e.g. a future sidebar badge + a page body), each call will issue a fresh DB round trip unless wrapped:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\nThis is the standard fix for the classic Next.js \"N+1 across components in one request\" pattern before it happens — cheap to add now, and it composes correctly with `countSourcesBySide` calling `listSources` internally.\n\n### 3. `getDb()` connection pool — no leak, but pool size is implicit\n`lib/db/index.ts:38` calls `postgres(url, { prepare: false })` without a `max` connections setting, so it falls back to the driver default (10). That's fine for now, but worth setting explicitly once you're near serverless/edge deployment (each cold-start-heavy environment can multiply effective connections against Supabase's pooler limits). Not a bug today — flagging for when `runs`/`conflicts` pages add heavier concurrent load.\n\n### 4. React side — nothing actionable yet\n`NavRail` (`components/console/nav-rail.tsx`) re-renders on every route change via `usePathname()`, but it only maps over 5 static nav items and does cheap string comparisons — memoizing would be premature optimization with no measurable payoff. No `useEffect`, no manual subscriptions, no uncled-up timers/listeners anywhere in the client components I read (`landing-hero.tsx`, `pipeline-strip.tsx`, `step-list.tsx`, `get-started-hero.tsx`, `sources-empty-state.tsx`) — Framer Motion's `useReducedMotion` manages its own `matchMedia` listener cleanup internally, so no leak there either.\n\n### What to revisit as the app grows\nThe real N+1 risk in this codebase will show up once `runs`, `entities`, `matches`, and `conflicts` pages exist — those tables are FK-linked (`runs → entities → matches → conflicts → resolutions`) and a naive \"for each run, fetch its conflicts\" implementation is the classic trap. When you build those pages, use Drizzle's relational query API (`db.query.runs.findMany({ with: { ... } })`) or explicit joins rather than per-row fetches. Happy to review that code when it lands.\n",
